{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "בזונו לנו", :translit "bazunu lanu", :gloss "our spoil for us"} {:he "בזזנו לנו", :translit "bazaznu lanu", :gloss "we plundered for us"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay pivoting on בזזנו (root בזז, 'we plundered') becoming בזונו (root בזה, 'despised'), reversing the middle consonant from ז to ו — swapping the final two letters to turn 'we plundered' into 'it was despised.'"}], :frame {:text "The lexical move depends on the phonetic and orthographic proximity of two roots: בזז (bāzal, 'to spoil/plunder'; noun בִּזָּה in Deuteronomy 3:7) and בזה (bāzah, 'to despise'; e.g., Genesis 25:34, 'and Esau despised the birthright'). Ba'al HaTurim exploits the near-anagram to suggest a causal link: the very abundance of plunder (בזז) bred contempt (בזה).", :sources ["Genesis 25:34" "Deuteronomy 3:7"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay pivoting on בזזנו (root בזז, 'we plundered') becoming בזונו (root בזה, 'despised'), reversing the middle consonant from ז to ו — swapping the final two letters to turn 'we plundered' into 'it was despised.'", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:33:39.160849642Z", :model "glm-5.2"}